course: reflection
lesson: 24
title: I failed: the missed penalty, the lost race, the bad mark
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Failing in front of people gets replayed like a video: Ollie missed the penalty that would have won the cup final, the other team went wild, nobody spoke to him in the changing room, and for a week in bed he saw the ball going wide: "Bottom left. I always go bottom left. Why did I change? I lost it for everyone."
explain: That night he cried into his pillow so his brother wouldn't hear; for two weeks he didn't want to touch a football and hid his boots under the bed; after a month his coach asked him to take a penalty in training, and he scored; he still thinks about the final, but it has become a memory, not a wound.
explain: What he slowly understood: one kick feels like it decided everything, but the team had a whole match of chances; even famous footballers miss huge penalties and come back; and his mind replays it to learn from it, but the hundredth replay teaches nothing new.
explain: His coach said, "Only people brave enough to take penalties miss them," and watching famous misses online helped; hiding his boots did not.
explain: Failure stings because you cared; the trick is to hear "not yet" instead of "never", to look at what you learned, and to measure yourself against your own last try, not against the friend who passed.
fact: One mistake at the end of a game feels like it decided everything, but a result comes from the whole match and the whole team.
fact: Famous players have missed huge penalties, for example Roberto Baggio in the 1994 World Cup final, and went on to great careers.
fact: Replaying a failure can help you learn at first, but endless replays at night just keep the hurt fresh.
fact: "Gutted" means very disappointed; "bottled it" means lost your nerve; "unlucky, mate" is a kind thing to say after a miss.
fact: A test result is one day's performance, not a measure of your whole brain; many people thrive at the school they did not plan for.
fact: Not getting a part or not being picked once does not decide the future; there are other roles, and practice and time change a lot.
fact: Effort that ends in a collapse or a loss is not wasted; the skills and learning stay with you.
fact: Losing a vote does not mean nobody likes you; elections depend on many things, and helping the winner can feel good.
fact: Everyone learns at their own speed; comparing yourself with others who finished first tends to make it feel worse.
fact: A low mark on work you were proud of can feel unfair; feedback means advice on what to improve, and asking for it helps.
fact: A personal best is your best result ever; beating it is real progress even if you came last.
fact: Some things take longer to learn, and a different way of practising, like a song for times tables, can help; "not yet" is truer than "my brain doesn't work".
exercise: Ollie missed the cup final penalty and thinks, "I lost it for everyone." What is true? | The team had a whole match of chances, not just one kick | It really was all his fault and nobody else's | The other team only won because he is bad at football and always was | Missing a penalty means he should never play again
exercise: How did Ollie's feeling change over a month? | It became a memory, not a wound | From fine to much worse, and he quit football | From angry to happy the very next morning | It stayed exactly the same for the whole month
exercise: What did Ollie's coach mean by "Only people brave enough to take penalties miss them"? | Missing shows he was brave enough to try | Brave people never miss a single penalty | He should never take a penalty again | Only cowards miss penalties in finals
exercise: What did NOT help Ollie? | Hiding his boots under the bed | Watching famous misses online | Taking a penalty in training | His coach's kind words after training
exercise: Mia failed the entry test her best friend passed. What helped her? | Visiting her new school and finding a club she liked | Calling herself stupid every night until she started believing it | Avoiding her best friend for the whole year | Taking the test again every week until she passed
exercise: Sam didn't get a part in the school play. What helped? | Joining the backstage crew | Quitting drama forever | Saying the play was rubbish | Asking for the lead part next week
exercise: Ava's science project collapsed just before judging. What helped? | The judge letting her explain it anyway | Blaming her partner for everything in front of the judges | Throwing the whole project away | Pretending it never collapsed
exercise: Kofi wasn't picked for the county team, but his friend was. What helped? | A training plan from his coach | Giving up football forever | Telling his friend he cheated | Never speaking to the coach again
exercise: Lily lost the school election and thinks, "Nobody likes me." What is true? | Losing a vote doesn't mean nobody likes her | Losing the vote means the whole school secretly hates her | Only unpopular people lose elections | She should never stand for anything again
exercise: What made Lily proud of herself afterwards? | Helping the winner | Sulking for a week | Asking for a recount | Starting a rival club
exercise: Noah was the only one who didn't finish his swimming badge. What is true? | Everyone learns at their own speed | He will never be able to swim | He must be the worst swimmer ever | Badges only go to the cleverest kids
exercise: Jess got a low mark on a story she was proud of. What helped? | Asking her teacher what to improve | Tearing the story up and throwing it in the bin | Deciding she's bad at writing | Never writing a story again
exercise: What is feedback? | Advice on what to improve | A mark out of ten | A kind of punishment for bad work | Feeding back your lunch
exercise: Archie trained for months and came last. What did his coach show him? | He had beaten his own best time | He was the slowest runner in the world | Training never makes any difference | He should switch to a different sport
exercise: Ruby couldn't learn her times tables after weeks. What helped? | A song for each table | Crying over flashcards every night | Giving up on maths for good | Copying a friend's answers
exercise: Which phrase is truer than "my brain doesn't work"? | Not yet | Never ever | Too late | Totally hopeless
test: Ollie's teammate mutters, "he bottled it, man." ("Bottled it" means lost his nerve.) What would a good teammate say instead? | "Unlucky, mate, we had loads of chances" | "Yeah, you lost us the whole cup, cheers for that" | "You should never take penalties" | "Good, now we know who to blame"
test: What would happen if Ollie replayed the miss a hundred more times in bed? | It would keep the hurt fresh without teaching him anything new | He would score the penalty in his dream and so feel he had won the cup | His memory would delete the penalty forever | The final would be replayed in real life
test: Mia's nan from Glasgow says, "Dinnae fash yersel', hen, it's one test." ("Dinnae fash yersel'" means don't worry yourself.) What's the point? | One test doesn't measure her whole brain | Tests don't matter at all, ever | She should do the test again tomorrow | She shouldn't care about her best friend
test: Sam's mate goes, "backstage is where the mid kids go." ("Mid" means not good.) What's the truer view? | Backstage is a real part of the show and he enjoyed it | Backstage is only for people who are bad at drama and can't act | He should have refused and quit drama | Only the lead actor matters in a play
test: Ava's partner says, "it's a disaster, back to the drawing board." ("Back to the drawing board" means start again.) What did Ava learn? | The effort still taught her a lot | Working hard is pointless if things can fall apart | It was entirely her partner's fault | She should never do a science project again
test: Kofi thinks, "I'm not good enough. I'll never be." What happened after he trained harder? | He kept improving | He stayed exactly as good as before | He decided football wasn't for him | He got picked the very next day
test: Lily's mate goes, "you only lost cos you're not popular, ngl." ("Ngl" means not gonna lie.) What's true? | Elections depend on many things, not just being liked | She lost because nobody in school likes her | Only popular kids should stand in elections | Losing proves she was a bad candidate
test: Noah's cousin from Brisbane says, "Heaps of kids take ages, mate, you'll get it next term." ("Heaps" means lots.) What actually happened? | Noah got the badge next term after extra lessons | Noah gave up swimming completely and never went back to the pool | Noah got the badge the next day | Noah was told he can never swim
test: Jess thinks: "Maybe I'm just bad at writing." What did asking her teacher show? | The story could be improved, and the rewrite got a better mark | She really is bad at writing and should stop trying to write stories | Teachers never explain marks to pupils | The mark was given by mistake
test: In a cartoon, the hero trains for a montage and wins the race every time. What does Archie's story add? | You can train hard, come last, and still beat your own best | Training always guarantees first place | If you come last, all the months of training were completely wasted | Only the winner's effort counts for anything
test: Ruby shouts, "my brain's literally broken!" after another bad times table test. What's the better thought? | "Not yet; I need a different way to practise" | "My brain is broken and will never work" | "Times tables are pointless anyway" | "I should give up maths altogether"
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the significance of a single missed penalty in the outcome of a match? | The result is determined by the entire match and the entire team | The result is determined solely by the final penalty taken | The penalty taker bears sole responsibility for any defeat | A missed penalty precludes any future participation in sport
exam: Identify the principal reason for which repeated nocturnal recollection of a failure ceases to be beneficial. | Beyond initial learning, further repetition merely sustains distress | Repetition invariably alters the recorded result of the match | Recollection is prohibited during the hours of darkness | Recollection during the night reduces one's physical fitness considerably
exam: It is given that a pupil finished last in a race but achieved a personal best. Select the response consistent with the established facts. | The pupil has made genuine progress notwithstanding his position | The pupil's training is deemed to have been wholly wasted | A personal best is recognised solely where first place is attained | The pupil ought to discontinue the sport forthwith
exam: In respect of the term "feedback" as applied to written work, determine which description is correct. | Advice concerning the means by which the work may be improved | A numerical grade awarded without any comment | A penalty imposed for unsatisfactory performance | The return of the work unread to the pupil
